--TEST--
`Core\Process\Result::exitCode` answers the status the child exited with, named on both sides of the bound — one program started twice, once to succeed and once to fail — `rule:core-classes/process-run`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:core-classes/process-run` writes `$result->exitCode()`, and this is the only case in which
// a run *completes*: every other `Core\Process` case is refused before a child
// starts, so a member that answered `0` always — or that widened a boolean, or
// that answered "did it run" rather than "what did it say" — reads as correct
// against all of them.
//
// Both sides are asserted together for that reason. `0` is the answer an
// unwritten member gives; `3` is a number nothing but the child can have
// chosen, so the pair pins that the status comes back from the operating system
// rather than out of the shape of the result object.
//
// The child is this platform's own shell, named absolutely and started as an
// ordinary program with a split argv. § 1 says a `PATH` lookup is the caller's
// own to make, so there is no name here to look up, and § 4's refusal tells a
// caller who wants an interpreter to "start that interpreter yourself if it is
// what you mean" — what the ADR closes is a command line *Novis* builds, and
// nothing here builds one. The two candidates are the two families CI runs on,
// and the one that is not this platform's starts nothing. On Windows `/bin/sh`
// starts at the drive's root and nothing is there, so the operating system
// answers `IOError`. On Unix `C:\Windows\System32\cmd.exe` starts at no root, so
// `Core\Process::run` throws the `RuntimeError` every relative path gets
// (`rule:programs/relative-paths-resolve-from-their-file`) before it reads a
// grant. `IOError` is a `RuntimeError`, so the Windows block's one clause
// catches both.

// `-2` and not `-1`: a signalled child answers `-1`, so a sentinel spelled that
// way could not be told apart from a real status.
int $started = 0;
int $absent = 0;
int $success = -2;
int $failure = -2;

try {
    var $good = Core\Process::run("/bin/sh", ["-c", "exit 0"]);
    var $bad = Core\Process::run("/bin/sh", ["-c", "exit 3"]);
    $started = $started + 1;
    $success = $good->exitCode();
    $failure = $bad->exitCode();
} catch (IOError $notAUnix) {
    $absent = $absent + 1;
}

try {
    var $goodOnWindows = Core\Process::run("C:\\Windows\\System32\\cmd.exe", ["/c", "exit 0"]);
    var $badOnWindows = Core\Process::run("C:\\Windows\\System32\\cmd.exe", ["/c", "exit 3"]);
    $started = $started + 1;
    $success = $goodOnWindows->exitCode();
    $failure = $badOnWindows->exitCode();
} catch (RuntimeError $notWindows) {
    $absent = $absent + 1;
}

// The two counts come first because they are what make the two statuses mean
// anything: on every platform exactly one candidate is there and exactly one is
// not, so a run where nothing started would print the sentinels below and read
// like a platform difference rather than like a broken member.
echo "started=", $started, "\n";
echo "absent=", $absent, "\n";
echo "success=", $success, "\n";
echo "failure=", $failure, "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# `true`, not a list of roots: what this case asserts is the status a completed
# child exited with, and the door's own scoping is already pinned by
# `process-run-asks-for-the-capability-before-it-looks-at-the-target.nvst`. A
# root list would have to survive canonicalisation on three platforms as well,
# which is a second claim this case does not make.
[capabilities.process]
exec = true
--EXPECT--
started=1
absent=1
success=0
failure=3
